    "body": "Intramotev will deploy two autonomous freight railcars on Missouri’s Columbia Terminal Railroad as part of a project supported by a $16.2 million federal grant awarded to the City of Columbia.\nThe Federal Railroad Administration selected the city for funding under its Consolidated Rail Infrastructure and Safety Improvements (CRISI) program, according to the announcement. Intramotev will serve as the technology partner, deploying its TugVolt technology on COLT’s 21.4-mile line between Centralia and Columbia.\nOperations will proceed in phases under an FRA-approved test program, beginning with remote switching at COLT’s Columbia terminal and advancing to autonomous freight service across the full mainline, including its public crossings. The grant also will fund track and infrastructure upgrades.\n“Short lines keep America’s small cities and rural communities connected to the freight network,” said Tim Luchini, Intramotev’s chief executive. “Autonomous railcars can help them serve more customers, more often, at lower cost, and this program will show what that looks like on a working mainline.”\nCOLT is a municipally owned short line operated by Columbia City Utilities. Intramotev will build both TugVolt units at its St. Louis facility.\n“COLT has been Columbia’s link to the national rail network for nearly 40 years, and the businesses that depend on it support hundreds of local jobs,” said Erin Keys, the city’s utilities director. “This federal investment lets us modernize the railroad, strengthen service for our customers, and put Columbia at the forefront of rail innovation.”\nThe project is intended to produce a safety record that FRA and the more than 600 U.S. short-line railroads can use as a template for adopting autonomous railcars, according to the announcement. It did not specify a deployment date or a timetable for moving from terminal switching to full-line service.\nThe award follows other developments for Intramotev this year. In April, the company announced an agreement with Austria’s OBB Rail Cargo Group. In June, U.S. Army Reserve soldiers trained on TugVolt technology during a planned technology assessment.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nUnion Pacific CFO to retire\nCSX eliminates more than 100 management positions\nIntermodal rail better by 10% in latest count CSX operations chief to retire State loan to aid Louisiana port’s new rail connection\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
